    {
      "number": 8,
      "id": "I_kwDOU6xPE88AAAABVORqIg",
      "title": "Is this actually true? \"Track's Properties are known before any of its Objects\"",
      "url": "https://github.com/afrind/draft-frindell-moq-timestamp/issues/8",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "sharmafb",
      "authorAssociation": "NONE",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "Is this actually true? For example, if someone sends a publish message along with objects in parallel, then can't the objects arrive before the request? ",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-05T21:34:23Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-31T15:29:04Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-10-31T15:29:04Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "afrind",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "@sharmafb yes the objects can arrive before the PUBLISH but the receiver wouldn't know which track these objects belong to until the PUBLISH establishes the track alias.  So effectively the properties are known by the time the receiver can do anything useful with the objects.",
          "createdAt": "2026-10-06T19:07:12Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-10-06T19:07:12Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "afrind",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "It holds today. A receiver can't tell which Track an Object belongs to until the Track Alias is established, and the same SUBSCRIBE_OK or PUBLISH that establishes the alias carries the Track Properties. An Object that arrives first waits in a buffer until the alias resolves, and by then its Track's Properties are known.\n\nThe draft could state that dependency explicitly instead of asserting the ordering. Something like: \"A receiver interprets an Object's timestamp using the Track Properties received with the message that associates the Object with its Track.\"\n",
          "createdAt": "2026-10-30T01:20:49Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-10-30T01:20:49Z"
        }
      ]
    },
