    {
      "number": 10,
      "id": "I_kwDOU6xPE88AAAABZUNtRw",
      "title": "Define a CLOCK_ID for TAI",
      "url": "https://github.com/afrind/draft-frindell-moq-timestamp/issues/10",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "gwendalsimon",
      "authorAssociation": "NONE",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "The draft defines one clock: CLOCK_ID 0, POSIX time excluding leap seconds. Would it be possible to add a defined CLOCK_ID for TAI (International Atomic Time, a continuous time scale with no leap seconds)? Its time 0 would be 1970-01-01T00:00:00 TAI. We would use it with OBJECT_TIMESTAMP to carry the emission time of each Object in MPEG-2 transport stream tracks (see [draft-gregoire-moq-msfts](https://github.com/mondain/msfts))",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-23T07:37:40Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:32Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:32Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "number": 12,
      "id": "I_kwDOU6xPE88AAAABbET7eA",
      "title": "CLOCK_ID text left over from before #4",
      "url": "https://github.com/afrind/draft-frindell-moq-timestamp/issues/12",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "afrind",
      "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "PR #4 made a missing CLOCK_ID mean POSIX time, and any present value a clock unrelated to wall-clock time. Some text still uses the old meaning, where 0 was wall clock and a missing CLOCK_ID left the timeline unplaced:\n\n- The CLOCK_ID IANA entry says \"0 identifies wall-clock time, and other values identify shared clocks.\"\n- The Fixed Cadence, Explicit Timestamps, Group IDs as Timestamps, and Timeline Template examples all set `CLOCK_ID = 0` for wall-clock tracks. Read literally, those tracks are now on a clock unrelated to wall-clock time.\n- The CLOCK_ID definition says Tracks with the same *non-zero* CLOCK_ID share a clock. It doesn't say what a present CLOCK_ID of 0 means.\n- For on-demand content, the Timeline Template example says the publisher \"omits CLOCK_ID and TIMESTAMP_ORIGIN.\" That now places the content at 1970 on wall-clock time, rather than leaving it unplaced. The shared-clock alternative in the same sentence still works. The omission option should go, or #6 should settle what an unplaced timeline looks like.\n\n#10 already reads the draft as \"CLOCK_ID 0 = POSIX.\"\n",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-30T01:20:47Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:31Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:31Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "number": 13,
      "id": "I_kwDOU6xPE88AAAABbET8BA",
      "title": "A wall-clock track can't use TIMESTAMP_ORIGIN",
      "url": "https://github.com/afrind/draft-frindell-moq-timestamp/issues/13",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "afrind",
      "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "\"A Track that carries TIMESTAMP_ORIGIN without CLOCK_ID is malformed.\" Since #4, a missing CLOCK_ID is how a track says it's on wall-clock time, so a wall-clock track can't carry an origin. Every Object then carries a full epoch timestamp, about 8 bytes at microsecond resolution, when a delta from the origin would take 2\u20133.\n\nThe Security Considerations text (\"omit CLOCK_ID (and hence the origin)\") also assumes the old model.\n\nThe fix is probably to drop the rule and let a missing CLOCK_ID with an origin mean POSIX time plus that origin.\n\nWe hit this implementing the draft in moxygen's moqtest publisher. It has to send microseconds since the epoch on every Object.\n",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-30T01:20:48Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:29Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-10-31T12:55:29Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
